  "title": { "en": "Matatu & Getting Around", "so": "Matatu iyo Socodka" },
  "swTitle": "Matatu na Usafiri",
  "description": {
    "en": "Ride a matatu, ask for directions, get off at the right stop and move around Nairobi like a local.",
    "so": "Fuul matatu, weydii jihada, ka deg joogsiga saxda ah oo Nairobi ugu soco sida dadka deegaanka."
  },
  "goals": ["travel"],
  "guide": [
    {
      "title": { "en": "How matatus work", "so": "Sida matatu-yadu u shaqeeyaan" },
      "body": {
        "en": "Matatus are shared minibuses with fixed routes and numbers. You wait at a stage. The konda (conductor) calls the destination, collects the fare (nauli) and tells the driver when someone wants to get off.",
        "so": "Matatu-yadu waa baasas yaryar oo la wadaago oo leh waddooyin iyo lambarro go'an. Waxaad ku sugtaa stage (joogsiga). Konda (kirishboy) ayaa ku dhawaaqa meesha loo socdo, qaada lacagta safarka (nauli), una sheega darawalka marka qof doonayo inuu dego."
      },
      "examples": [
        { "sw": "Nauli ni pesa ngapi?", "en": "How much is the fare?", "so": "Lacagta safarku waa immisa?", "v": "everyday" },
        { "sw": "Nishukishe hapa.", "en": "Drop me here.", "so": "Halkan i deji.", "v": "everyday" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": { "en": "Kenya vs Tanzania on the road", "so": "Kenya iyo Tanzania waddada" },
      "body": {
        "en": "Kenyans say matatu, stage, jam and boda boda. In Tanzania you will hear daladala, kituo and foleni. To get off, Kenyans say Nishukishe hapa, while Tanzanians say Nishushe hapa.",
        "so": "Kenyaanku waxay yiraahdaan matatu, stage, jam iyo boda boda. Tanzania waxaad maqli doontaa daladala, kituo iyo foleni. Si loo dego, Kenyaanku waxay yiraahdaan Nishukishe hapa, halka Tansaaniyaanku yiraahdaan Nishushe hapa."
      },
      "examples": [
        { "sw": "Niko kwa jam.", "en": "I'm stuck in traffic.", "so": "Ciriiriga gaadiidka ayaan ku jiraa.", "v": "everyday" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": { "en": "Directions", "so": "Jihooyinka" },
      "body": {
        "en": "Kulia is right, kushoto is left, moja kwa moja is straight on. Kenyans usually say enda for 'go' (the textbook form is nenda) and pinda for 'turn'.",
        "so": "Kulia waa midig, kushoto waa bidix, moja kwa moja waa toos. Kenyaanku badanaa 'soco' waxay ku yiraahdaan enda (qaabka buugaagtu waa nenda), 'leexo'na pinda."
      },
      "examples": [
        { "sw": "Enda moja kwa moja, kisha pinda kulia.", "en": "Go straight, then turn right.", "so": "Toos u soco, kadibna midig u leexo.", "v": "everyday" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": { "en": "Matatu culture", "so": "Dhaqanka matatu-yada" },
      "body": {
        "en": "Nairobi matatus are famous for loud music and bright art. Young people call the flashiest ones nganya. The conductor is often called makanga and the driver dere. These Sheng words are for friends; with older people use konda and dereva.",
        "so": "Matatu-yada Nairobi waxay caan ku yihiin muusig cod dheer iyo farshaxan midab leh. Dhallinyaradu kuwa ugu qurxoon waxay ugu yeeraan nganya. Kirishboyga badanaa waxaa loo yaqaan makanga, darawalkana dere. Erayadan Sheng waa saaxiibbada; dadka waaweyn la isticmaal konda iyo dereva."
      }
    }
  ],
